The fix for #311 is committed and queued to land, but the issue stays open, blocked by #455. Its second acceptance criterion (`run.sh` passes) can't be checked until #455 is fixed, so no patch release or backports were made.

**The change:** commit `001c3bf3` adds the comment `// trace: unrelated: gate driver; the call starts here, no inbound request to propagate` above the four calls in `cross-test/server-runtime/client/go/main.go`. It changes comments only, no behaviour.

**What I checked:**
- **`looper-trace check .`:** all four calls now show as unrelated. Its finding count drops from 9 to 1, and the remaining one (the outbound-deadline Rust fixture) is already tracked by #310.
- **`just gates`:** passed on this commit; all 16 gates returned 0 (`OVERALL fail=0`).
- **The land:** queued with the manager at 15:11Z. Its verdict hadn't arrived when the run ended, so the hash on main and the gate set it ran aren't known yet; the manager will post them on the issue.

**What I couldn't check:** `bash cross-test/server-runtime/run.sh` fails on main whether or not this change is in. The Go client build fails because the generated model lacks the client packages the driver imports. That is #455, which is open and approved. The cause is that the gate's `tacplus.yaml` lacks `grpc-servers: all`. With that line added, the gate then trips on a 2.6 `(spec.redact)` deprecation warning, which it treats as an error. I posted both findings on #455. As a substitute, I generated the model with `grpc-servers: all` and built the changed driver against it by hand; `go build` and `go vet` both pass.

**Release:** main has taken features since v2.6.1, so the patch has to come from `release/2.6` as a backport rather than from main. All seven release lines (2.0 to 2.6) have the same four unmarked calls, and each line's .0 tag is under twelve months old. I held the backport pull requests and patch tags until #455 lands, so each one can be checked the same way. The outcome file says `Blocked-by: #455`, and the issue comment covers all of this.
